% exp_ids: phase2_scierc_full_s1, llama_scierc_full_s1, phase2_docred_full_s1, llama_docred_full_s1, mistral_scierc, mistral_docred
\paragraph{Results.}
RV and ET reach $\leq$0.03\% for all models on both datasets.
DR drops 76--99\% on SciERC and 42--90\% on DocRED; R-F1 improves in 5/6 settings.
CF direction depends on calibration: S1 introduces CF for calibrated models (up to 12.02\%; \S\ref{sec:discussion}) but reduces it for Mistral (56.87\%\,$\to$\,1.27\%); fine-tuning resolves both (\S\ref{sec:composability}).
Post-hoc filtering achieves DR${=}$0\% but at 15--74\% rejection rates (Appendix~\ref{app:posthoc-extended}).

% Appendix: 70B-Class LoRA Ablation on SciERC
% exp_ids: eval_70b_lora_scierc_unconstrained, eval_70b_lora_scierc_grammar_only,
%          eval_70b_lora_scierc_semantic_only, eval_70b_lora_scierc_s1_constrained,
%          eval_70b_lora_scierc_s2_constrained, eval_70b_lora_scierc_beam4_s1

\paragraph{70B-class fine-tuned ablation.}
\label{app:70b-ablation}

Table~\ref{tab:70b-ft-ablation} presents the constraint ablation on Qwen2.5-72B-AWQ with LoRA fine-tuning on SciERC (551 documents).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3.6pt}
\begin{tabular}{@{}l rrrr rr@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Mode}
  & \textbf{DR\,$\downarrow$} & \textbf{RV\,$\downarrow$} & \textbf{ET\,$\downarrow$} & \textbf{CF}
  & \textbf{R-F1\,$\uparrow$} & \textbf{E-F1\,$\uparrow$} \\
\midrule
Unconstrained & 1.50 & 0 & 0 & 0    & 44.73 & 64.86 \\
Grammar       & 1.50 & 0 & 0 & 0    & 44.63 & 64.82 \\
Semantic      & 0.60 & 0 & 0 & 9    & 44.73 & 64.86 \\
S1            & \textbf{0} & 0 & 0 & 15 & 44.53 & 64.76 \\
S2            & \textbf{0} & 0 & 0 & 18 & 36.28 & 62.35 \\
Beam4 S1      & \textbf{0} & 0 & 0 & \textbf{1} & \textbf{44.81} & \textbf{64.89} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{70B-class (Qwen2.5-72B-AWQ) LoRA ablation on SciERC (551 docs).
CF: violation count. F1 $\times$100.}
\label{tab:70b-ft-ablation}
\end{table}

The grammar layer provides zero DR reduction (1.50\%\,$\to$\,1.50\%), consistent with the zero-shot 70B pattern where fine-tuned models already produce structurally valid JSON (\S\ref{sec:70b-results}).
The semantic layer is the sole effective defense (1.50\%\,$\to$\,0.60\%, 60\% reduction) with no R-F1 cost ($\Delta{=}$0\,pp), and full S1 eliminates all DR.
S2 eliminates DR but causes severe R-F1 degradation ($-$8.45\,pp), indicating that optimal constraint granularity is scale-dependent: the additional S2 constraints become overly restrictive at 70B, where stronger baseline generation makes fine-grained intervention counterproductive.
Beam ($k{=}4$) S1 remains the optimal configuration (DR${=}$0\%, CF${=}$1, R-F1${=}$44.81).

% Runtime overhead (inline paragraph, no subsection header to save space)

\paragraph{Runtime overhead.}
\label{sec:runtime}
S1 overhead is $+$13.1\% on SciERC, lower on DocRED due to amortization over longer sequences; under LoRA, $+$10.0\% with 37\% lower absolute latency.
Constraint compilation is one-time $<$0.5\,s; scaling benchmarks confirm constant overhead (2--3\%) up to 200 entity types and 1{,}000 relations (Appendix~\ref{app:scalability}).

\paragraph{Beam search compatibility.}
\label{sec:beam-results}
